Hi Chris

 

Thanks for your reply.

 

It was definitely the twin cable. The overall picture is kind of acceptable
but suffers from double conversions. 

 

I had no interference or problems as such until the temp goes up a few
degrees. 

 

Best wishes for the year.

 

If I was totally happy with the picture quality (when cold) I would have
said yes to a replacement and give that a go but the picture is like looking
at an old analogue TV and watching a good VCR recording in Betamax. Better
than a grainy VHS but still not quite what one expects.

 

Regards

 

Cornelis 

 

From: Chris Bailey [mailto:converters@converters.tv] 
Sent: Saturday, 29 December 2018 3:51 PM
To: Cornelis
Cc: sales@converters.tv
Subject: Re: further analysis of converter

 

Hi Cornelis,

Great work and no problem to sort when I return to work on Wednesday Jan 2.

One last question, which apparently was an initial problem when product was
first packaged and it concerns the 9 pin cable.

I am unsure if the cable was double-checked before being shipped.

Is the cable a single or twin tail construction? Twin tail has inputs in one
lead and outputs in the other.

If single tail, then signal interference issues occur.

Looking forward to your reply.

And also, Happy New Year !

Best Regards,

Chris Bailey

 

 

 

On 29/12/2018 6:34 pm, Cornelis wrote:

Hi Chris

I borrowed a scope from a friend. This is what the Sync looks like with the
jumper in (on the circuit board) in the alternative location (not as
supplied) and the unit operating at around 20 degrees C or below. Although
the sync frequency is about 1 kHz higher than required the LCD display syncs
well when the circuit board is "cold" with putting out a steady 16.6 kHz.

 

 

60 Hz
NTSC pulse should not make a difference..5
V/div=2.6 V0-pk20
us/div= 60us/cycle=16.6 kHz

 

 

With the jumper in the location (as supplied) the image is a bit of a mess,
so I didn't bother taking a picture of the oscilloscope display only took a
video to illustrate it. 

 

However if the unit warms up the oscilloscope display is pretty much the
same as if you remove the jumper and insert in the original location. As you
see on the video the sync then becomes non-existent and has little or no
amplitude and gets a mess. See video.

 

This is the picture of the board. I cannot see any heatsinks fitted as such.



 

The unit has been reassembled and packed ready to be returned. 

 

Although I am returning the unit in a re-sellable condition complete with
all packaging and paperwork I do not believe it should be re-sold unless no
sync is required.

 

I think I would opt for a refund if you don't mind. Also because even when
"cold" the picture quality was not fantastic because of double conversions
(VGA to NTSC and this one NTSC to RGB/CGA).

 

Please provide a return address. 

 

Regards

 

Cornelis

 

 


 
